# useSend 1.9.8 environment schema (@env-spec / varlock format).
#
# The compose now pins usesend/usesend:v1.9.8 (it floated on `latest`). On
# 2026-09-04 (research date) v1.9.8 is the newest stable release, published
# 2026-08-30, and `latest` resolves to the same manifest
# (sha256:2b2939e1…), so the float was not stale, but it is now pinned.
#
# THE PROJECT WAS RENAMED. Unsend became useSend: github.com/usesend/useSend,
# image usesend/usesend. The old docker.io/unsend/unsend repo is ABANDONED:
# its newest tag is v1.5.5, pushed 2025-07-27, and `latest` there still points
# at that 1.5.x build. This template already used the new repo; nothing to
# move.
#
# COMPOSE CHANGE THIS RELEASE REQUIRED: v1.9.8 ships "fix: docker host
# binding" (PR #438), whose actual fix is a compose edit. Upstream's
# docker/prod/compose.yml now sets HOSTNAME=0.0.0.0. The image is a Next.js
# standalone build, and Next's generated server.js does
# `const hostname = process.env.HOSTNAME || '0.0.0.0'`. Docker injects
# HOSTNAME=<container id> into every container, so without the override the
# server binds to the container's own name instead of all interfaces and
# login (and everything else) breaks behind published ports. This compose
# now sets it too. No other release between the old float and v1.9.8
# required a compose change.
#
# Source: apps/web/src/env.js at v1.9.8, a @t3-oss/env-nextjs + zod schema
# validated at boot (the image sets SKIP_ENV_VALIDATION="false") and therefore
# the authoritative key list, plus a `process.env.` grep across apps/web
# and packages/ for the handful of reads that bypass it (LOG_LEVEL, PORT,
# SKIP_ENV_VALIDATION, and the AWS_ACCESS_KEY / AWS_SECRET_KEY aliases that
# env.js resolves in its own runtimeEnv block). Cross-checked against
# .env.example, docker/prod/compose.yml and docs.usesend.com/self-hosting.
#
# Notable gaps between the code and the surrounding material:
#  - API_RATE_LIMIT is declared and parsed by env.js and set by upstream's
#    compose, but NOTHING in 1.9.8 reads the parsed value. It is inert.
#  - The docs still tell you to copy `.env.selfhost.example`; that file does
#    not exist in the v1.9.8 tree (only `.env.example`, which is the local
#    development one and is missing NEXTAUTH_SECRET's real requirement,
#    GITHUB_ID/GITHUB_SECRET, REDIS_KEY_PREFIX, the S3_COMPATIBLE_* block,
#    ADMIN_EMAIL/FOUNDER_EMAIL and EMAIL_CLEANUP_DAYS).
#  - Not declared here because they are build- or test-time only and never
#    operator input: DOCKER_OUTPUT (next.config.js, build), NEXT_RUNTIME (set
#    by Next itself), RUN_INTEGRATION (vitest), VERCEL / VERCEL_URL (env.js
#    only consults them on Vercel, which this is not).
#  - The `apps/smtp-server` SMTP proxy is a SEPARATE service upstream and is
#    not part of this stack, so its own variables (SMTP_AUTH_USERNAME,
#    USESEND_BASE_URL, USESEND_API_CERT_PATH, USESEND_API_KEY_PATH) are not
#    declared. SMTP_HOST/SMTP_USER below are only the strings the dashboard
#    displays.
#  - SES itself is NOT configured by environment beyond credentials and a
#    default region: region, callback URL and sending rate are entered in
#    the UI on first login and stored in Postgres.

# @defaultSensitive=false @defaultRequired=infer
# @docs("Self hosting useSend", https://docs.usesend.com/self-hosting/overview)
# ---

# ─── Core ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# PLATFORM-OWNED. Interface the Next.js standalone server binds to. Docker
# sets HOSTNAME=<container id> in every container and Next uses it verbatim
# (`process.env.HOSTNAME || '0.0.0.0'`), which is why v1.9.8 pins it to
# 0.0.0.0. Without this the app listens on the wrong address and nothing can
# reach it through the published port.
# @required
# @docs("Docker setup for useSend", https://docs.usesend.com/get-started/set-up-docker)
HOSTNAME=0.0.0.0

# The public URL the instance is served at, scheme included. Every OAuth
# callback, magic-link and campaign/unsubscribe link is built from it, and
# it is also the address you give SES/SNS for delivery callbacks. Prompted at
# install.
# @required @type=url
USESEND_URL=

# PLATFORM-OWNED: the same value, handed to NextAuth as its canonical origin
# for sign-in callbacks and CSRF checks. env.js validates it as a URL and the
# app refuses to boot without it. A mismatch with the browser's address is
# the usual cause of an OAuth "redirect_uri" failure.
# @required @type=url
# @docs("NEXTAUTH_URL", https://next-auth.js.org/configuration/options#nextauth_url)
NEXTAUTH_URL=${USESEND_URL}

# Port the standalone server listens on. Blank is safe: Next parses it and
# falls back to 3000, which is the port this compose exposes. Changing it
# without also changing the exposed port breaks routing.
# @type=port
PORT=

# PLATFORM-OWNED. Marks this as a self-hosted instance rather than useSend
# Cloud. It has real security weight: with it false, `adminProcedure`
# (server/api/trpc.ts) lets EVERY signed-in user reach the admin surface (SES
# settings, team administration), so on a self-hosted box "who has an
# account" is the whole access-control story (see the invite gate below).
# It also disables all billing/plan limits. Next bakes NEXT_PUBLIC_* into the
# client bundle at build, where it is already "false"; this value only
# changes server-side reads.
# @type=boolean
NEXT_PUBLIC_IS_CLOUD=false

# Node environment. The generated standalone server.js hard-assigns
# `process.env.NODE_ENV = 'production'` before the app loads, so this cannot
# be changed from outside the container and env.js's own "development"
# default never applies here. Left empty for that reason.
# @type=enum(development, test, production)
NODE_ENV=

# Pino log level for the server logger (server/logger/log.ts). Empty means
# "info" on this image. Set it to a REAL level or not at all: a present but
# blank value would be an invalid level, which is why it is not pre-filled.
# @type=enum(fatal, error, warn, info, debug, trace, silent)
# @docs("Pino logger levels", https://github.com/pinojs/pino/blob/main/docs/api.md)
LOG_LEVEL=

# Escape hatch that skips the zod validation of this entire environment at
# boot. The image deliberately sets it to "false" so a misconfigured
# instance fails loudly instead of half-starting; it also short-circuits the
# "no auth providers configured" check in server/auth.ts. Do not set it.
SKIP_ENV_VALIDATION=

# Version strings shown in the dashboard footer. Baked into the image at
# build time from the release tag and git sha (Dockerfile ARGs), so setting
# them here only changes server-side reads, not the client bundle.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_VERSION=
NEXT_PUBLIC_GIT_SHA=

# ─── Bundled Postgres and Redis ───────────────────────────────────────────

# PLATFORM-OWNED. Prisma's connection string, pointing at the bundled
# Postgres. `prisma migrate deploy` runs against it on every container start
# (docker/start.sh) before the server boots, so the database must be
# reachable or the container exits.
# @required @sensitive @type=url
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://usesend:${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}@${{stack.postgres.HOST}}:5432/usesend

# PLATFORM-OWNED. BullMQ's connection, used for the email send queue,
# campaign batching, the daily retention job and the sign-in rate limiter.
# The bundled Redis runs with `--maxmemory-policy noeviction` because BullMQ
# refuses to work reliably under eviction. Required: env.js has no default.
# @required @type=url
REDIS_URL=redis://${{stack.redis.HOST}}:6379

# Namespace for every key useSend writes, if you point REDIS_URL at a Redis
# you share with other apps. Empty (the default) means unprefixed keys and a
# `bull` queue prefix; set to e.g. `usesend` and keys become `usesend:*`.
# Changing it after the fact orphans queued jobs.
REDIS_KEY_PREFIX=

# PLATFORM-OWNED. The bundled Postgres role and database, matching
# DATABASE_URL above.
POSTGRES_USER=usesend
POSTGRES_DB=usesend

# Password for that role, used by both the database and DATABASE_URL.
# Generated at install (`openssl rand -base64 24`) and prompted for.
# @required @sensitive
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# ─── Auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Signs and encrypts NextAuth's session JWTs and CSRF tokens. Required,
# because server.js forces NODE_ENV=production, and env.js makes this a hard
# requirement there, so the container will not start without it. Anyone who
# learns this value can MINT A SESSION FOR ANY ACCOUNT on the instance,
# including the first (admin-equivalent) user; never reuse it across
# deployments or paste it into an issue. Rotating it signs everyone out.
# Generate with `openssl rand -base64 32`. Prompted at install.
# @required @sensitive @type=string(minLength=32)
# @docs("NEXTAUTH_SECRET", https://next-auth.js.org/configuration/options#nextauth_secret)
NEXTAUTH_SECRET=

# GitHub OAuth app credentials, the login method the self-hosting guide
# assumes. Both must be set or the provider is not registered, and useSend
# throws "No auth providers found" at boot if NO provider is configured
# (GitHub, Google, or FROM_EMAIL). Callback URL:
# `<USESEND_URL>/api/auth/callback/github`; grant the app read-only access to
# the user's email addresses, which is how invitations are matched. Prompted
# at install.
# @sensitive
# @docs("Creating an OAuth app", https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/creating-an-oauth-app)
GITHUB_ID=
# @sensitive
GITHUB_SECRET=

# Google OAuth alternative to GitHub. Both halves required together; the
# callback is `<USESEND_URL>/api/auth/callback/google`. Like GitHub, this
# provider is registered with `allowDangerousEmailAccountLinking: true`, so
# an account is matched by verified email address across providers.
# @sensitive
# @docs("Using OAuth 2.0 to access Google APIs", https://developers.google.com/identity/protocols/oauth2)
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# @sensitive
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# WHO CAN SIGN UP. On a self-hosted instance (NEXT_PUBLIC_IS_CLOUD=false)
# registration is not open: server/auth.ts's `canRegisterSelfHostedUser`
# lets the FIRST account through on an empty install (the bootstrap admin,
# whoever reaches the login page first), and after that only an email with a
# pending team invitation, or an already-linked account, may sign in. So the
# window to claim the instance is between first boot and your first login;
# do not leave a fresh instance reachable and unclaimed. There is no
# environment switch that re-opens signup.
#
# ADMIN_EMAIL is NOT that gate. It only sets the `isAdmin` session flag and
# is enforced solely when NEXT_PUBLIC_IS_CLOUD is true; on this stack it
# changes nothing about who can administer the instance. It doubles as a
# fallback From address for admin-sent mail.
# @type=email
ADMIN_EMAIL=

# Magic-link sign-in requests allowed per client IP per 60 seconds
# (app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts, counted in Redis). 0 (the default)
# disables the limiter entirely. Only relevant if the email provider is
# active, i.e. FROM_EMAIL is set.
# @type=number
AUTH_EMAIL_RATE_LIMIT=0

# ─── AWS SES / SNS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The IAM user's credentials, and the blast radius is the reason to treat
# them as production secrets: with them useSend can SEND MAIL AS ANY IDENTITY
# VERIFIED IN YOUR ACCOUNT, create and delete SES domain identities and
# configuration sets, and create SNS topics and subscriptions. The
# self-hosting guide tells you to attach AmazonSESFullAccess and
# AmazonSNSFullAccess, so a leak of these is a leak of your whole sending
# reputation and of anything else the attached policy happens to permit.
# Scope the policy down and rotate on exposure. Both must be set or neither
# (server/aws/credentials.ts throws on a half-set pair); omitting both falls
# back to the SDK's default provider chain (instance role). Prompted at
# install.
# @sensitive
# @docs("Create AWS credentials", https://docs.usesend.com/get-started/create-aws-credentials)
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# @sensitive
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# Legacy aliases env.js still honours (`AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID || AWS_ACCESS_KEY`).
# Use the canonical names above; these exist only so old Unsend-era
# deployments keep working.
# @sensitive
AWS_ACCESS_KEY=
# @sensitive
AWS_SECRET_KEY=

# Default SES region for new domains and for sending when a domain has no
# region of its own. Prompted at install; env.js falls back to `us-east-1`
# when unset, and v1.9.6 added rejection of regions SES does not serve.
# Region choice is per-account sending reputation, so changing it later
# means re-verifying domains.
# @docs("SES endpoints and quotas", https://docs.aws.amazon.com/general/latest/gr/ses.html)
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=

# Override endpoints for the SESv2 and SNS clients, for pointing at a
# local/emulated AWS (upstream's own dev stack) or a VPC endpoint. Empty
# means the real regional AWS endpoints.
# @type=url
AWS_SES_ENDPOINT=
# @type=url
AWS_SNS_ENDPOINT=

# ─── Mail the app sends about itself ──────────────────────────────────────

# From address for useSend's OWN mail: sign-in magic links, team invitations,
# admin notices. Setting it also REGISTERS THE EMAIL SIGN-IN PROVIDER, so it
# is one of the three ways to satisfy the "at least one auth provider"
# requirement, but it delivers through this instance's own SES setup, which
# does not exist until you have logged in once and verified a domain. Bootstrap
# with GitHub or Google, then add this. The address's domain must be one of
# your verified domains or the send falls back to `hello@<first domain>`.
# @type=email
FROM_EMAIL=

# API key for a DIFFERENT useSend instance (or useSend Cloud) used as the
# transport for the app's own mail instead of local SES. UNSEND_API_KEY is
# the pre-rename alias, still read as a fallback.
# @sensitive
# @docs("API reference", https://docs.usesend.com/api-reference/introduction)
USESEND_API_KEY=
# @sensitive
UNSEND_API_KEY=

# Cloud-flavoured extras that still work self-hosted: FOUNDER_EMAIL is the
# reply-to on welcome mail, CONTACT_BOOK_ID adds new signups to a contact
# book through USESEND_API_KEY, and DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL receives internal
# notifications. All optional; each feature is simply skipped when empty.
# @type=email
FOUNDER_EMAIL=
CONTACT_BOOK_ID=
# @sensitive @type=url
DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL=

# ─── SMTP proxy (display only) ────────────────────────────────────────────

# The host and username shown on the dashboard's SMTP page as the credentials
# to point a mail client at. They are STRINGS RENDERED IN THE UI (nothing
# connects to them), and they default to useSend Cloud's proxy. If you run
# upstream's separate `apps/smtp-server` alongside this stack, set them to
# that service's address so the page stops advertising the hosted one.
# @docs("SMTP support", https://docs.usesend.com/get-started/smtp)
SMTP_HOST=smtp.usesend.com
SMTP_USER=usesend

# ─── Image uploads (S3-compatible) ────────────────────────────────────────

# Optional object storage for images pasted into templates and campaigns.
# All four of KEY/SECRET/API_URL/PUBLIC_URL must be set or `isStorageConfigured()`
# returns false and the editor simply hides image upload, with no error and
# no fallback. API_URL is the S3 endpoint (path-style, so MinIO works),
# PUBLIC_URL is the base the browser fetches uploaded images from. BUCKET
# defaults to `unsend` if left empty; it is not created for you.
# @sensitive
S3_COMPATIBLE_ACCESS_KEY=
# @sensitive
S3_COMPATIBLE_SECRET_KEY=
# @type=url
S3_COMPATIBLE_API_URL=
# @type=url
S3_COMPATIBLE_PUBLIC_URL=
S3_COMPATIBLE_BUCKET=

# ─── Retention and limits ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Days of stored email content to keep. Unset (the default) keeps message
# bodies FOREVER, and base64 attachments are the heaviest column in the
# database. Set a positive number and a midnight-UTC job clears `text`,
# `html`, `attachments` and `headers` on older rows (v1.9.8 added the last
# two); delivery status and event history are always kept.
# @type=number
# @docs("Email content retention", https://docs.usesend.com/self-hosting/overview)
EMAIL_CLEANUP_DAYS=

# Parsed by env.js (default 1) and set by upstream's compose, but no code
# path in 1.9.8 reads the parsed value; API rate limiting is per-API-key in
# the database, not from this. Inert; left at the code default.
# @type=number
API_RATE_LIMIT=1

# ─── Cloud-only (inert on this stack) ─────────────────────────────────────

# Stripe wiring for useSend Cloud's metered billing. Every consumer of these
# sits behind NEXT_PUBLIC_IS_CLOUD, which this compose sets to false, so on a
# self-hosted instance they do nothing. Declared because env.js still reads
# them and a blank value is what keeps billing off.
# @sensitive
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# @sensitive
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
STRIPE_BASIC_PRICE_ID=
STRIPE_BASIC_USAGE_PRICE_ID=
STRIPE_LEGACY_BASIC_PRICE_ID=
